Yes, you should absolutely pressure/load test your Node.js application before migrating from localhost to Render. Testing ensures your app can handle real-world traffic, identifies memory leaks, and reveals bottlenecks before they affect users, especially since Node.js can easily queue too many requests if not managed. 

Key Reasons to Pressure Test:
Identify Breaking Points: Determine how much traffic (RPS/RPM) your chosen Render instance can handle.
Prevent Crashes: Detect uncaught exceptions and check if your app restarts properly.
Optimize Resources: Ensure your package.json engines, build scripts (npm run build), and environment variables are properly configured for production.
Verify Performance: Test database interaction and API latency. 

Steps to Take Before Migrating:
Use Testing Tools: Run tests using tools like Artillary, Loadtest, or autocannon on your local environment to simulate load.
Ensure Graceful Shutdowns: Implement handling for SIGTERM / SIGINT signals so your app shuts down gracefully when Render shuts down a container.
Optimize package.json: Ensure your engines are defined and scripts are optimized.
Database Consideration: Note that local tests might not reflect performance over a network, so testing with a similar database environment (e.g., PostgreSQL on Render) is ideal. 
